WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.300
- One of the questions I get all the time

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:05.180
is how do you get into law school.

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:06.960
Everyone has a different route,

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:08.510
but today I thought I'd tell the story

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:09.740
of how I got into one

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:12.328
of the best law school's in the country.

00:00:12.328 --> 00:00:14.911
(bright music)

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.290
Hey legal eagles, D. James Stone here,

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:21.490
teaching you how to think like a lawyer

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:23.500
so you can crush law school.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:25.800
Now before you can crush law school,

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.110
you have to get into law school.

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:30.760
Now I have been a practicing litigator

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.660
for over 10 years now,

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:35.060
but before that I had to go to law school

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:36.660
just like everybody else.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.100
And so here is my story.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:41.870
I knew from a very early age

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:44.580
that I wanted to be a lawyer.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.160
There was a brief period of time

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:48.950
when I thought I wanted to be a policeman,

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:51.310
but when I learned that police officers

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:53.240
are not allowed to carry machine guns,

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:56.120
I didn't really want to become a policeman

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:57.450
after all.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:58.910
There was a short period of time

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:00.840
when I wanted to be an architect,

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.810
but that didn't last too long,

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:07.390
mainly because I started
to see lawyers on TV.

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:09.910
I was really into the
TV show, Law and Order.

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:11.300
Now this is back in the day

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:13.450
with the original Law and Order series,

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:15.860
not the SVU or any of the off-shoots,

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:18.220
when they would split up the episode

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:20.660
into half that was for police

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:22.720
and then half for lawyers.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:24.680
And I always thought the latter half

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:25.690
of the episode,

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:27.140
that focused only on the lawyers,

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:28.520
was always the best part,

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.310
and I knew I wanted to be a lawyer

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:32.180
like I saw on TV.

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:33.860
There was only one problem.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.560
My parents put a hard limit

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:38.710
on how much TV I could watch.

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:41.080
I think I was only able
to watch 30 minutes

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:42.130
of recreational TV,

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:44.890
but I was able to watch
an unlimited amount

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:47.380
of educational TV.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:49.890
So in one of my first acts

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:53.850
of pure legal argument,
I convinced my parents

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.493
that because I wanted to be a lawyer,

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:00.600
really Law and Order was educational TV

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.130
and I should be allowed to watch

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:04.740
as much Law and Order as I wanted.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.640
For some reason, this
argument carried the day,

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:09.310
and my parents agreed.

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:12.530
And this is back in the
day when TBS or TNT,

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:14.670
one of those cable channels,

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:16.550
would just show Law and Order,

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:17.710
basically non-stop.

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:21.200
I mean, these were almost
during the daytime,

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:22.920
the Law and Order channel.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.490
And so I had a chance to watch

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:27.070
quite a bit of Law and Order

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:30.280
and that provided the goal

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.960
for what I wanted to do with my career.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:34.930
I think my parents assumed two things

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:36.070
based on my arguments.

00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:37.830
Number one, I think they assumed

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.590
that if I was willing to make

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:41.133
these kind of arguments

00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:43.860
that I was really interested in the law,

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:45.827
and so what harm could it do?

00:02:45.827 --> 00:02:47.460
And number two, that they figured

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:49.780
I was pretty good at arguing.

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:52.120
So that led into high school,

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:55.890
where I did mock trial in high school.

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:57.550
I was really mad because my freshmen year,

00:02:57.550 --> 00:02:59.579
they would not let freshmen compete

00:02:59.579 --> 00:03:01.220
on the mock trial team.

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:03.563
So I had to wait until my sophomore year.

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:06.910
I graduated from high
school, went to college.

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:09.900
And I chose the college that I went to

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:12.930
because they had one
of the best collegiate

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:15.220
mock trial teams in the entire country.

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:18.250
And ,in fact, while I
was on the college team,

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:21.780
we won the national
championship twice in a row.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:23.990
In college, I majored in poli-sci

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:25.600
mainly because I was interested,

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:28.900
but it really seemed like so many lawyers

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:32.340
had also majored in political science.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.880
So I figured if other lawyers

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:37.600
majored in poli-sci, it
probably couldn't hurt.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:40.240
A lot of people ask what majors

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:43.620
they should take in college.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:44.900
They see the same thing,

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:47.020
that lots of lawyers take poli-sci.

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:49.680
I would say that the poli-sci degree

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:51.750
was really interesting for me.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:53.010
Because it was interesting,

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:54.900
I put a lot of effort into it,

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.470
and so I got good grades.

00:03:56.470 --> 00:03:58.710
But it definitely didn't really help

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:02.220
in terms of preparing
for law school itself.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:03.850
Just because you know about government

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:08.390
doesn't mean that you
know what the laws are,

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:09.390
and it certainly doesn't mean

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:11.030
that you're able to think like a lawyer

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:12.020
just because you know

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:13.822
some of the history of governance

00:04:13.822 --> 00:04:16.430
and maybe even some political theory.

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:20.623
So while I really enjoyed
my poli-sci degree,

00:04:21.710 --> 00:04:23.940
I don't really think it
helped me all that much

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:25.870
in law school.

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:28.510
But frankly, no major really helps you

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:30.950
that much in law school.

00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:34.000
Really what you should
do is just the major

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.620
that you are most interested in,

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:37.040
so that you can get good grades

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:39.260
because law schools look at your grades,

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:41.530
and that helps decide

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:43.980
what law school you can go to.

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:45.040
Now that being said,

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:47.260
I did take a number of electives

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:49.620
that did end up being
helpful for law school,

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:52.520
one of which was a business class

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:55.530
that was taught by my mock trial coach.

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:58.440
My mock trial coach was
not only a professor

00:04:58.440 --> 00:04:59.960
at the undergraduate level,

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:01.500
but he was also a professor

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:04.120
at the business school.

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:07.770
And he happened to teach
business law classes.

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:09.710
These were mainly for business students,

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:11.270
but undergrads could take it.

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:14.360
And while it wasn't a great survey

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.660
of all the different
types of subsets of law

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:20.250
that are out there that
you learn in law school,

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:21.870
it did provide a framework

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:25.490
for what we call civil
procedure in law school.

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:27.630
In law school, you have six core classes,

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:29.970
one of which is called Civ-Pro,

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:33.180
and that's the process of filing a lawsuit

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:35.260
and how to file motions

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:36.880
and basically everything that leads up

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:40.080
to going to trial in a lawsuit.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:42.500
And so that gave me a framework

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:44.810
for that specific class
in Civil Procedure.

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:46.330
So I'm really happy

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:49.480
that I took that Business Law class.

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:52.240
I did pretty well, in
general, in undergrad.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:56.690
I graduated Summa Cum
Laude, Phi Beta Kappa.

00:05:56.690 --> 00:05:59.940
And I was able to graduate with honors

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:02.320
from UCLA undergrad.

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:04.460
Now I knew that when I graduated,

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:06.840
I immediately wanted to go to law school.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:09.230
I couldn't afford to do a gap year

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:11.290
and to travel the world,

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:12.175
and I knew that

00:06:12.175 --> 00:06:14.410
I didn't wanna enter the workforce

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:16.710
because that would just be a waste of time

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:19.010
because I really wanted to be an attorney.

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:20.570
So I didn't wanna mess around,

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:22.810
I wanted to go straight into law school.

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:24.910
So basically, from the entire time

00:06:24.910 --> 00:06:28.260
that I graduated from college,

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:29.930
for the entire summer

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:32.220
and a good portion of the fall,

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:34.040
I studied for the LSAT.

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:35.240
And I think I might have started

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:37.785
studying for the LSAT
during the school year

00:06:37.785 --> 00:06:39.400
to get a head start.

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:41.800
The LSAT is to law schools

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:43.750
what the SAT is for college.

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:46.200
It's your law school entrance exam.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.710
It really is the single
most important thing

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:52.090
that you can do to get
into a good law school,

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:55.490
is to get a really high LSAT score.

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:59.880
And the LSAT is scored from zero to 180.

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:01.930
So if you get a perfect score on the LSAT,

00:07:01.930 --> 00:07:03.600
you get a 180.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:04.760
The LSAT is scored,

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:06.850
I think, using a normal distribution.

00:07:06.850 --> 00:07:09.560
So really only the top

00:07:10.476 --> 00:07:12.990
.1 or .5% get a 180.

00:07:12.990 --> 00:07:14.880
If you get a 170,

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:17.220
that means you're in the 99th percentile.

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:19.370
And if you get a 160 or so,

00:07:19.370 --> 00:07:21.470
you're about in the 90th percentile,

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:24.820
and 10's of thousands of college students

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:26.370
take the LSAT every year.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:29.020
So I had taken a couple of practice tests,

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:32.360
practice LSAT's just to see how I scored.

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:34.660
And I scored really low

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:37.320
just with no studying whatsoever.

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:40.120
So I knew that I needed
to take a prep course

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:41.530
in order to boost my score

00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:44.220
to get into a good law school.

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:45.820
There are several good options

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:46.840
out there right now.

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:49.813
I highly recommend live courses.

00:07:49.813 --> 00:07:53.700
The ones that force you
to go into the class

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:54.890
and force you to study

00:07:54.890 --> 00:07:57.440
as opposed to the ones
that are self-directed

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:00.390
because it's so important

00:08:00.390 --> 00:08:02.170
that you really have to squeeze out

00:08:02.170 --> 00:08:04.180
every point you can

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:05.940
and I just didn't trust myself

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:07.520
to study on my own,

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:09.970
at least to learn the strategies.

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:14.140
I took a course with a
company called Test Master

00:08:14.140 --> 00:08:15.870
that was based out of California

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:18.070
and I think they've
expanded nationally now.

00:08:18.070 --> 00:08:19.730
I took their live class

00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:21.370
and I was actually instructed

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:23.560
by one of the instructors who would go on

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:26.770
to form the competitor to Test Master's

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:28.220
called Blueprint.

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:29.890
And I think those instructors

00:08:29.890 --> 00:08:31.030
were just phenomenal.

00:08:31.030 --> 00:08:33.440
The original Test Masters instructors

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:35.640
and also the ones that defected

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:37.820
and went off to Blueprint.

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:39.700
I think they are really phenomenal.

00:08:39.700 --> 00:08:42.370
So I always recommend those two companies,

00:08:42.370 --> 00:08:44.246
and especially their live courses.

00:08:44.246 --> 00:08:46.450
They're really pricey,

00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:50.600
I mean on the order of 1500 to $2000,

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:52.894
but the instruction that you get

00:08:52.894 --> 00:08:54.620
is just really good

00:08:54.620 --> 00:08:57.050
and it's an investment in your future.

00:08:57.050 --> 00:08:59.100
You can't get into a good law school

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:02.950
without a really stellar LSAT score.

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:05.560
So I think it's just a
worthwhile investment.

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:07.180
It's expensive, but I think

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:08.120
you just have to do it.

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:12.490
After I took the live
class with Test Masters,

00:09:12.490 --> 00:09:14.640
I then basically buckled down

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:16.540
with my college roommate

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:21.210
and we practiced taking LSATs every day

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:24.640
for about two and a half months.

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:28.560
I mean, we just we
studied all day, every day

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:32.500
and by the end of the summer, we had taken

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:35.342
every single publicly available LSAT

00:09:35.342 --> 00:09:36.780
that had been published.

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:40.710
So that was on the order
of hundreds of LSATs.

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:44.190
And both he and I were scoring

00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:46.260
in the 99th percentile.

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:48.500
He averaged, I think, a 174,

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:50.393
and I averaged a 172.

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:54.853
So I went into the LSAT
feeling very confident.

00:09:56.010 --> 00:09:58.900
Unfortunately, the day I took the LSAT,

00:09:59.740 --> 00:10:01.190
still can't really explain it,

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:02.740
but I scored significantly lower

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:04.990
than I had in practice.

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:07.060
I scored in the mid-160s.

00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:09.850
So that still pisses me off to this day,

00:10:09.850 --> 00:10:11.420
but the score was high enough

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:13.820
that I could apply to most of the schools

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:14.980
that I wanted to apply to

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:17.090
and have a reasonably good shot

00:10:17.090 --> 00:10:19.250
at getting into a few of them.

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:21.470
So I applied to pretty much every school

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:24.700
in the top 20 and some of the schools

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:26.560
that were outside of the top 20,

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:28.580
but were regionally very good.

00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:30.840
I knew, for personal reasons,

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:33.940
that I wanted to stay in Los Angeles.

00:10:33.940 --> 00:10:37.630
So I was really, really
looking to get into UCLA.

00:10:37.630 --> 00:10:39.580
I was also looking as USC.

00:10:39.580 --> 00:10:43.330
I also looked at Loyola and Pepperdine.

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:46.210
I considered some East Coast schools,

00:10:46.210 --> 00:10:48.810
some Northern California schools,

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:51.210
and I basically just applied everywhere

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:53.860
in the top 20's, hoping that a few of them

00:10:53.860 --> 00:10:56.100
would stick against the wall.

00:10:56.100 --> 00:10:58.220
And that summer,

00:10:58.220 --> 00:10:59.540
I got into a few

00:10:59.540 --> 00:11:00.770
and I was wait-listed at a few.

00:11:00.770 --> 00:11:05.430
I got wait-listed at
Cornell, Duke, and UCLA.

00:11:05.430 --> 00:11:08.430
I got into Loyola and Pepperdine.

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:10.780
So I knew I could stay in Los Angeles

00:11:10.780 --> 00:11:11.823
if I wanted to.

00:11:13.517 --> 00:11:16.590
My study partner, my
roommate, scored a 172

00:11:17.540 --> 00:11:19.810
on his exam or it mighta been a 170,

00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:23.200
so he was safely within
the 99th percentile

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:25.670
of the LSAT and he got into Stanford

00:11:25.670 --> 00:11:26.970
through early admission.

00:11:26.970 --> 00:11:28.722
So good for him.

00:11:28.722 --> 00:11:30.540
(laughs)

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:32.110
But I played wait-list game

00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:33.273
for most of the Summer.

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:37.590
And for personal reasons,
I wanted to stay in LA.

00:11:37.590 --> 00:11:41.810
So I was really hoping
to get into UCLA and USC.

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:42.880
Now that being said,

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:47.200
since I was an Undergrad UCLA Bruin,

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:49.720
I was really, really reluctant

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:52.500
about going to USC.

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:55.900
Nothing against the
school, it's a fine school.

00:11:55.900 --> 00:11:59.090
It's just that UCLA
Bruins hate USC Trojans

00:11:59.090 --> 00:12:00.120
and vice versa.

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:03.450
So I decided I would,

00:12:03.450 --> 00:12:06.070
even though I got into USC,

00:12:06.070 --> 00:12:08.410
I was still playing the wait-list game

00:12:08.410 --> 00:12:10.640
over the summer, hoping
that I could get in

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:13.330
off of the wait-list into UCLA.

00:12:13.330 --> 00:12:16.820
So eventually a few weeks
before school started,

00:12:16.820 --> 00:12:18.467
I was looking for apartments close

00:12:18.467 --> 00:12:20.120
to the USC campus.

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:23.250
I had resigned myself to going to USC

00:12:23.250 --> 00:12:24.870
in downtown LA.

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:28.310
And I actually signed
a lease in Hollywood.

00:12:28.310 --> 00:12:29.240
I figured that was

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:32.740
a reasonably close
neighborhood to downtown LA.

00:12:32.740 --> 00:12:35.473
And it was the day that I signed the lease

00:12:35.473 --> 00:12:38.070
for an apartment in Hollywood

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:41.360
that I got the call from UCLA admissions

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:43.070
letting me know that I got in

00:12:43.070 --> 00:12:45.140
off of the wait-list.

00:12:45.140 --> 00:12:47.360
I did a few tactics

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:49.110
to get in off of the wait-list.

00:12:49.110 --> 00:12:53.570
So I was pestering the
UCLA admissions department

00:12:53.570 --> 00:12:55.290
probably more than I should have,

00:12:55.290 --> 00:12:57.260
but my efforts paid off.

00:12:57.260 --> 00:12:59.690
I got in to UCLA.

00:12:59.690 --> 00:13:01.710
And that's where I decided to go.

00:13:01.710 --> 00:13:03.920
I graduated from UCLA Law,

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:05.490
stayed in the LA area

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:09.250
and became a practicing attorney in LA.

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:11.680
Now the story of how I
did well in law school

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:13.580
will have to wait for another day.

00:13:13.580 --> 00:13:14.750
But if you wanna hear more

00:13:14.750 --> 00:13:16.480
about my experience in law school

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:19.150
and my experience as a
practicing litigator,

00:13:19.150 --> 00:13:20.460
check out this quick
playlist (funky music)

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:21.293
I've put together.

00:13:21.293 --> 00:13:24.370
It includes a bunch legal war stories.

00:13:24.370 --> 00:13:25.880
And so just click on that playlist

00:13:25.880 --> 00:13:27.630
and I'll see you in the next video.

